  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "Pre-Assessment",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Pre-Assessment",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "designing-reliable-apps-with-ai-and-apis-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 1: Where Data Comes From",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Where Data Comes From",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How does an app get information from somewhere beyond its own code?**\n\nThis lesson introduces how apps retrieve live data from external servers by having students explore APIs, JSON, and the request/response model.  Students investigate real API calls, navigate documentation, and recognize the tradeoffs of building apps that depend on external services.",
        "preparation": "- Give each student a copy of the Where Data Comes From activity guide",
        "student_overview": "**How does an app get information from somewhere beyond its own code?**\n\nLearn how apps get live data from servers by exploring API calls, API documentation, JSON, and the request/response model."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 1: Where Data Comes From",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "designing-reliable-apps-with-ai-and-apis-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 2: Why Apps Wait",
      "name": "Lesson 2: Why Apps Wait",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**Why does my code keep running before the data has arrived?**\n\nThis lesson introduces asynchronous programming by focusing on what happens when apps have to wait. Students observe loading states, discuss real-world analogies, and explore simple JavaScript examples to trace a request, wait, and response. The emphasis is on recognizing why blocking code breaks user experience and where delays or failures can occur in that timeline.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Why Apps Wait activity guide (one for each student)\n- Gather highlighters (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**Why does my code keep running before the data has arrived?**\n\nExplore what happens when an app has to wait for something to load. You’ll look at loading states, compare different ways tasks can run, and try code that doesn’t freeze the screen."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 2: Why Apps Wait",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "designing-reliable-apps-with-ai-and-apis-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 3: Getting Data Online",
      "name": "Lesson 3: Getting Data Online",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What really happens when my code calls fetch?**\n\nThis lesson introduces how JavaScript handles waiting using Promises. Students model the states of a Promise through a real-world analogy, then trace what happens when `fetch()` is called, from request to response or error. The lesson emphasizes that `fetch()` returns a Promise—not data—and helps students identify where waiting, success, and failure occur in the timeline. By the end, students can explain why `.then()` and `.catch()` are needed to handle asynchronous results without freezing the interface.",
        "student_overview": "**What really happens when my code calls fetch?**\n\nLearn what happens when code has to wait. You’ll explore how `fetch()` starts a request, how Promises move through pending, fulfilled, or rejected states, and how `.then()` and `.catch()` handle the result. By tracing each step, you’ll see how apps stay responsive while waiting for data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 3: Getting Data Online",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "designing-reliable-apps-with-ai-and-apis-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 4: Making Steps Clear",
      "name": "Lesson 4: Making Steps Clear",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I write asynchronous code that reads like normal step-by-step code?**\n\nThis lesson deepens students’ understanding of asynchronous programming by introducing async and await. Students learn to refactor Promise-based fetch calls into clearer, top-to-bottom code, compare readability, and critically evaluate AI-assisted code refactoring.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies the Making Steps Clear activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can I write asynchronous code that reads like normal step-by-step code?**\n\nLearn how to use async and await to write asynchronous code that reads step-by-step and use to AI refactor existing code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 4: Making Steps Clear",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "designing-reliable-apps-with-ai-and-apis-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 5: Making Sense of Data",
      "name": "Lesson 5: Making Sense of Data",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do I turn JSON text into data I can loop over?**\n\nThis lesson helps students make sense of raw API data by focusing on structure, not volume. Students learn to recognize the shape of JSON, extract specific values using dot notation, and transform backend-shaped data into frontend-ready formats using `.map()`. The emphasis is on intentional data selection and transformation, reinforcing that apps rarely use all available data and that understanding structure is required before data can be used effectively in a UI.",
        "student_overview": "**How do I turn JSON text into data I can loop over?**\n\nIn this lesson, you’ll learn how to make sense of messy-looking data from an API. You’ll practice spotting patterns in JSON, pulling out one meaningful value, and reshaping data so it’s easier to use in an app. By the end, you’ll understand how developers turn raw data into clean information that works in a user interface."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 5: Making Sense of Data",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "designing-reliable-apps-with-ai-and-apis-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 6: Showing Data on the Page",
      "name": "Lesson 6: Showing Data on the Page",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do I turn a list of objects into visible cards on the page?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how apps turn raw JSON data into visible content. They plan out a music app by selecting which information from the JSON data to display and then coding a loop to dynamically display it. Finally, they learn to debug and refactor code in instances where the backend JSON object has been modified.",
        "student_overview": "**How do I turn a list of objects into visible cards on the page?**\n\nLearn how to turn JSON data into visible cards or lists on a webpage by building a dynamic, data-driven music app."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 6: Showing Data on the Page",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "designing-reliable-apps-with-ai-and-apis-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 7: When Things Break",
      "name": "Lesson 7: When Things Break",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do Reliability Engineers design systems that users can still trust when something breaks?**\n\nThis lesson introduces error handling as a design and engineering practice through the lens of a Reliability Engineer. Students analyze failure pages, work through support tickets from a concert ticketing app to diagnose common error types — network failure, server failure, and invalid data — and make tradeoff decisions between failing fast and failing quietly. Students then design recovery states that communicate clearly and maintain user confidence.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the When Things Break activity guide (one for each student)\n- (Optional) Print copies of the Designing for Failure activity guide",
        "student_overview": "**How do Reliability Engineers design systems that users can still trust when something breaks?**\n\nStep into the role of a Reliability Engineer for a concert ticketing app. You'll review support tickets, diagnose what type of failure caused each problem, and decide how the app should respond. Then you'll design a recovery message that gives users a clear next step, even when things go wrong."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 7: When Things Break",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "designing-reliable-apps-with-ai-and-apis-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 8: Catching Problems",
      "name": "Lesson 8: Catching Problems",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can try and catch keep my app from crashing?**\n\nThis lesson introduces `try...catch` as a core error-handling pattern for building resilient apps. Students learn to wrap asynchronous `fetch` logic in protective code, prevent crashes, and display fallback UI when failures occur. Through guided practice and challenge levels, the lesson emphasizes defensive design as a way to maintain user trust and keep applications functional even when external systems fail.",
        "student_overview": "**How can try and catch keep my app from crashing?**\n\nIn this lesson, you’ll learn how to protect an app when something goes wrong. You’ll use `try...catch` to safely handle errors, show friendly fallback messages, and keep the app from crashing. By the end, you’ll understand how defensive design helps apps stay reliable and trustworthy."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 8: Catching Problems",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "designing-reliable-apps-with-ai-and-apis-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 9: Keeping Users Informed",
      "name": "Lesson 9: Keeping Users Informed",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can my app communicate what it is doing so users do not guess?**\n\nThis lesson focuses on designing apps that clearly communicate with users by introducing loading, empty, and error states. Students design and implement visible feedback that builds trust and supports human-centered reliability.",
        "student_overview": "**How can my app communicate what it is doing so users do not guess?**\n\nLearn how to design loading, empty, and error states so your app clearly communicates what it’s doing instead of leaving people guessing."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 9: Keeping Users Informed",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "designing-reliable-apps-with-ai-and-apis-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 10: Local vs Live Data",
      "name": "Lesson 10: Local vs Live Data",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do Data Engineers design systems that keep apps running even when their data sources fail?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn about the Data Engineer career and take on the role as they examine the distinction between local data (stored in the app) and live data (retrieved from an external API). Through discussion and hands-on implementation, students analyze the tradeoffs between speed, reliability, freshness, and control, then design a hybrid app that combines both data sources using fallback logic to keep the application functional even when the external API fails.",
        "student_overview": "**How do Data Engineers design systems that keep apps running even when their data sources fail?**\n\nIn this lesson, you learn about the Data Engineer career and then take on the role — building a hybrid app that stays functional even when the live data source fails."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 10: Local vs Live Data",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "designing-reliable-apps-with-ai-and-apis-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 11: Explore-Style App Project",
      "name": "Lesson 11: Explore-Style App Project",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I build an app that shows real data and still feels dependable to the user?**\n\nThis project guides students through building an Explore-Style web app that fetches live data from an external API and reveals additional details through user interaction. Students implement async and await, attach event listeners, and render dynamic content based on API responses. They strengthen their apps with loading, error, and empty states to ensure the interface remains clear and trustworthy. AI functions as a collaborative debugging and refinement partner, with students documenting their logic and demonstrating ownership through code comments and a professional README.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Explore-Style  Project Planning Guide to students (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can I build an app that shows real data and still feels dependable to the user?**\n\nIn this project, you build an Explore-Style app that displays live API data and lets users click to reveal more information. You will use async and await, add interactive features, and design loading, error, and empty states so your app never feels broken. You will also use AI to help plan, debug, and refine your code while documenting how your app works and where its data comes from."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 11: Explore-Style App Project",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "designing-reliable-apps-with-ai-and-apis-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "name": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": true,
