  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "ceb9f8e6-d77f-4c1b-b342-5a806476208c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ceb9f8e6-d77f-4c1b-b342-5a806476208c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9ca3a0f5-0d3e-477d-93ae-d16debf98b85"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82a97c00-886e-4a75-9e58-909273ed20cb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students reason about the dynamic nature of apps and identify the need for external data sources to keep information current.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Look at this weather app. What information would likely change if you looked at this page tomorrow?*\n- *How does this page get that updated information?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that the weather data shown in the app changes over time and that the app itself isn't storing all of that future data. Guide students toward the idea that the app must be reaching out to some external source to get current information. Listen for responses like \"it connects to the internet\" or \"it updates automatically\" — these are the right instincts to validate and build on as the lesson moves forward.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82a97c00-886e-4a75-9e58-909273ed20cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3049ef3d-91c3-4b1f-8c5f-ea5fc44fd8be"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ac5ccc6-f381-4644-98d5-c4a8974bb11c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explore how apps communicate with external servers by first observing a live API call in action, then building vocabulary around APIs and JSON, and finally investigating real API documentation to see how data is structured and requested.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide. Use the diagram to connect back to the warm up — students just discussed that apps must reach out for data; this slide shows how that works. Point out that the phone holds only local data, and the server fills in the rest.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1 to explore the live weather app. Let students know they can click around and explore freely — if they haven't found the Network Tab on their own after a minute or two, give a light nudge by asking \"Is there anything in your browser tools that shows what the app is doing?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** While students explore the Network Tab, listen for observations like 'it looks like a list,' 'there are labels and values,' or 'it sent a request to a URL.' Do not define any terms yet. Validate curiosity and move to the class discussion.\n\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Where Does Data Live?",
        "progression_name": "Live API Exploration"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ac5ccc6-f381-4644-98d5-c4a8974bb11c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0dbce95-01af-47b5-9299-cc87319aa787"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f48cb39-5658-486f-8b90-a643978ee18c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**  Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* *What did you notice in the app or Network tab when the forecast refreshed?*\n* *What do you wonder about how the app got that weather data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface that the weather app displayed updated information and that something happened behind the scenes when students clicked Refresh Forecast. The goal is to get observations on the table before vocabulary is introduced — students don't need to define API, JSON, request, or response yet. Listen for students noticing that a new item appeared in the Network tab, that the app contacted a web address, or that the response data included weather information similar to what the app displayed. These observations become the foundation for the vocabulary and concepts introduced on the next slides.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students only noticed surface-level changes in the weather card, prompt them further by asking “What changed in the Network tab after you clicked Refresh Forecast?” or “Did you see anything that looked like the same weather information the app displayed?” This redirects attention to the behind-the-scenes data behavior without formally defining the terms yet.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f48cb39-5658-486f-8b90-a643978ee18c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0dbce95-01af-47b5-9299-cc87319aa787"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b2a757d-31b1-4374-9f3e-4bfd6d873bad",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Hand out Designing Reliable Apps with AI and APIs Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Define API.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the restaurant analogy: The API is the waiter who takes your request to the kitchen (server) and brings the food (data) back to you (client).\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b2a757d-31b1-4374-9f3e-4bfd6d873bad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0dbce95-01af-47b5-9299-cc87319aa787"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c847452-6d17-4c7f-969e-33ffc313a806",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This interaction is called the Request/Response model. The client asks, and the server provides.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c847452-6d17-4c7f-969e-33ffc313a806",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0dbce95-01af-47b5-9299-cc87319aa787"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f30fb3db-ae3d-4739-aca6-990267f2ae75",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Explain the relationship between the client and the server in the context of an API call. Connect the diagram back to what students observed in the exploration level — the app (client) sent a request out, and something came back. This slide names that pattern: the client asks, the server responds with data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define JSON.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that JSON is the 'language' APIs use to send data because it is structured and efficient for computers to process. It may help to frame it as a format or structure for organizing data, similar to how a spreadsheet organizes information in rows and columns.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f30fb3db-ae3d-4739-aca6-990267f2ae75",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0dbce95-01af-47b5-9299-cc87319aa787"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "23e5c97c-a485-4148-af71-cc3afce21465",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the annotated JSON diagram, pointing out each key:value pair, the curly braces, and how the structure maps to the bullet points on the left. Have students follow along in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give students a copy of the API Scavenger Hunt activity guide and the curated PDF documentation for the OpenWeather API.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are struggling to connect the structure to the concept, use this analogy: a key is like a label on a folder, and the value is the document inside it.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "23e5c97c-a485-4148-af71-cc3afce21465",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0dbce95-01af-47b5-9299-cc87319aa787"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b0906da5-0970-4625-9d8c-85b1c4bcb83c",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b0906da5-0970-4625-9d8c-85b1c4bcb83c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0dbce95-01af-47b5-9299-cc87319aa787"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "49c19b31-c206-4438-8185-99e0ac2aff25",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 4.\n- **Level 2:** Students view panel levels to learn about using the Networks Tab.\n- **Level 3:** Students use the Network Tab in Web Lab to find JSON data.\n- **Level 4:** Students compare AI’s explanation of the OpenWeather API against the actual documentation to verify claims about parameters and response data.\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Exploring the Network Panel",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck in the Network Tab, remind them to look for the 'Response' or 'Preview' sub-tabs to see the JSON clearly.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "49c19b31-c206-4438-8185-99e0ac2aff25",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0dbce95-01af-47b5-9299-cc87319aa787"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f1f09d4-00db-4dfc-9fbf-3ea0cee760ae",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Why do you think the designers of this weather app chose not to display all of the information in the Response Data file?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that apps receive far more data from an API than what gets displayed to users — and that designers make intentional choices about what to show based on what's useful or meaningful to the user. Listen for responses like \"some of it isn't useful to regular people\" or \"it would be too much information on the screen.\" Guide students toward the idea that this is a design decision: developers have to think about what data to request, what to display, and what to leave out. This connects directly to their checklist — they now have evidence of exactly which fields were used and which weren't.\n\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Navigate a Weather App"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f1f09d4-00db-4dfc-9fbf-3ea0cee760ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0dbce95-01af-47b5-9299-cc87319aa787"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ed6002c-c96b-4f1f-92cf-9a5a52569ce8",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students make their first live API call, using a structured framework to predict, test, investigate, and modify code — building a concrete understanding of how request parameters affect what data an app receives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the PRIM framework. Give students a moment to read through the four steps before moving to the level.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide. Introduce fetch() as the JavaScript command used to send a request to an API. Connect it back to the request/response model students just saw — fetch() is what kicks off that process from the app's side. Point out the Unit Guide annotation section and have students annotate the three steps as you walk through them.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "My First API Call",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Compare a fetch call to ordering food. The menu is the documentation, the order is the request, and the meal is the response data."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ed6002c-c96b-4f1f-92cf-9a5a52569ce8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0dbce95-01af-47b5-9299-cc87319aa787"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d18a4eb5-17c7-4748-aefe-1ea10804a2b3",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation. Explain the three-part pattern: `fetch(API_URL)` sends a request to the API, `.json()` reads the response as JSON, and `updateUI(data)` displays the returned data. Have students annotate the same code in their Unit Guide.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students are not expected to write or memorize this code — the goal is recognition and understanding of the pattern. If students seem anxious about the syntax, remind them that in upcoming lessons they'll have starter code to work from as well AI Tutor to help. \n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d18a4eb5-17c7-4748-aefe-1ea10804a2b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0dbce95-01af-47b5-9299-cc87319aa787"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b049d44-69a9-407f-ac87-c30372bacc2e",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 5 to complete Levels 5 through 7.\n* **Level 5:** Students choose an API and predict its output based on the code snippet.\n* **Level 6:** Students run the code, investigate with AI, and modify attributes to see new data.\n* **Level 7:** Students complete a check for understanding.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who are unsure which API to pick. Suggest PokeAPI for a structured experience or NASA for visual data. Watch for students who forget to click 'Run' after modifying code. Ask: 'What did you change in the request, and how did the response look different?'\n",
        "progression_name": "Make a Prediction",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 5–7 can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objectives. Look for students who can identify what the API URL is requesting (Objective 1), describe what changed in the response when they modified the request (Objective 2), and explain in their own words why changing the request changed what the server sent back — connecting that to the idea that apps depend on external services to function (Objective 3). \n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b049d44-69a9-407f-ac87-c30372bacc2e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0dbce95-01af-47b5-9299-cc87319aa787"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8c04fa7-5c0c-4b7f-8f61-8cdeba8440f3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answer using a specific example from the lesson — the API they explored, the JSON data they found in the Network tab, or what happened when the API called a server. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. Listen for students connecting the idea that apps use APIs to send requests to external servers, which respond with data the app couldn't store on its own.\n\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is short, skip the partner talk and do a quick whole-class share-out instead — collect two or three responses that capture different parts of the answer: one focused on what an API is, and one focused on what the data (JSON) looks like when it comes back. This ensures both key concepts from the lesson get named before students leave.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8c04fa7-5c0c-4b7f-8f61-8cdeba8440f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aec0366f-f55f-409b-9cbf-37cec021f881"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7c9b6be-3e82-4293-a434-2c00f701be6e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7c9b6be-3e82-4293-a434-2c00f701be6e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aec0366f-f55f-409b-9cbf-37cec021f881"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c7f7a336-8235-4ffe-88c6-2ea457d32953",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Activate prior knowledge of how apps behave offline to build system awareness — identifying what's built into an app versus what gets loaded from the internet.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c7f7a336-8235-4ffe-88c6-2ea457d32953",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf0d83b-7e14-4b3d-ac1d-4c5bcbd58438"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a052adfb-ec6a-45b6-acd3-ccf3c5ab4990",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Think about the last time you opened an app and it felt broken — maybe images didn't load or your feed was empty. Keep that in mind as we discuss.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a052adfb-ec6a-45b6-acd3-ccf3c5ab4990",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf0d83b-7e14-4b3d-ac1d-4c5bcbd58438"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4092984-8305-46ff-b8ac-468f8e506c3c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Imagine opening an app offline. What changes? What stays the same?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify that the app layout (buttons, icons, navigation) is still visible, but live data (songs, playlists, weather, news) is missing or replaced by loading indicators. Listen for students distinguishing between what's built into the app versus what gets fetched from somewhere else.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use student responses from the discussion to bridge into the formal terms. Point out that what students noticed as \"still there\" offline = Static Content, and what was missing = Dynamic Content. Define each category using the examples on the slide.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Reinforce the definitions with a physical analogy: Static Content is like the menu at a restaurant — it's printed and always ready. Dynamic Content is like the food — it has to be made and brought to you after you order. This analogy will carry forward into the Activity section, so introduce it here and invite students to hold onto it.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4092984-8305-46ff-b8ac-468f8e506c3c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf0d83b-7e14-4b3d-ac1d-4c5bcbd58438"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c85c3ce3-02eb-419e-882d-3d6b53adcbc7",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c85c3ce3-02eb-419e-882d-3d6b53adcbc7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf0d83b-7e14-4b3d-ac1d-4c5bcbd58438"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62cada79-a3e3-4a1f-ba07-ad132bf75830",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How does an app tell you it is \"thinking\" or \"waiting\"?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify visual cues like spinners, progress bars, skeleton screens, or gray placeholder boxes. Listen for students connecting these cues to the idea that the app knows it's waiting — it hasn't crashed, it's just loading something.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62cada79-a3e3-4a1f-ba07-ad132bf75830",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf0d83b-7e14-4b3d-ac1d-4c5bcbd58438"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95a20241-5ccb-4fc6-895c-ba4abe16274a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build an intuition for the inefficiency of doing tasks one at a time (sequentially) and the benefit of multitasking — setting up the concepts of synchronous and asynchronous execution before those terms are formally introduced.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "The Restaurant Analogy"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95a20241-5ccb-4fc6-895c-ba4abe16274a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f3ff2c3-0852-487a-b095-9033f583aeee",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Picture a chef who only does one thing at a time — puts water on to boil, then stands there and watches it until it's done, then starts chopping vegetables.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f3ff2c3-0852-487a-b095-9033f583aeee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "feb1200e-c667-430a-a3ef-c4f741341349",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How efficient is a kitchen where the chef boils water, waits, then chops veggies?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that waiting for each task to fully finish before starting the next is inefficient and frustrating for the customer. Listen for words like \"slow,\" \"wasteful,\" or \"it would take forever.\" These observations set up the contrast on the next slide.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What can a chef do while they are waiting for the water to boil to prepare the meal more efficiently?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify that the chef can multitask — chopping, prepping sauce, toasting bread — while the water heats. Listen for the key insight that starting something and coming back to it later makes the whole process faster. This mirrors how code can start a task and keep running while waiting for a response.\n\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Avoid introducing the terms \"synchronous\" and \"asynchronous\" here — students will encounter those formally after the Frozen App demo. For now, keep the language in the analogy space: \"waiting vs. doing other things while waiting.\" This preserves the explore-before-vocab sequence.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "feb1200e-c667-430a-a3ef-c4f741341349",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8dadf657-4b77-4fe6-917c-fd6d942fbbb1",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8dadf657-4b77-4fe6-917c-fd6d942fbbb1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "499754b7-fa50-4917-9d50-07bf2e02896b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students experience the problem of synchronous blocking firsthand in a simulated app, discuss what they observed, and then connect that experience to the formal definitions and code representations of synchronous and asynchronous programming.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Frozen Apps to Async Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "499754b7-fa50-4917-9d50-07bf2e02896b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d16d08e0-1207-47cf-b4d8-da7f51365fcb",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You are about to run an experiment in an app. Try interacting with it and pay attention to what happens when you trigger a heavy task. Does the app stay responsive, or does it ignore you?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d16d08e0-1207-47cf-b4d8-da7f51365fcb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "481986ea-58a8-4a22-bd32-694d68b1fea9",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1 to interact with the Frozen App demo and observe how a heavy task affects the user experience.\n\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Demo: The Frozen App"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "481986ea-58a8-4a22-bd32-694d68b1fea9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ffe792fd-7b5d-4b92-a020-a0607edb01c4",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Demo: Run the Frozen App"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ffe792fd-7b5d-4b92-a020-a0607edb01c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "58baaa9e-ab31-4a80-a278-9dcb58b67176",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n* *Which specific action was a synchronous operation?*  \n* *Which specific action was an asynchronous operation?* \n* *How did you know the difference just by looking at the screen?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for students connecting what they observed on screen to the user experience. For the Like button, students should notice that the app responded right away and they could keep interacting. For the Compress Video button, students should mention that the app froze for several seconds and ignored other clicks. Listen for phrases like “The app stopped responding,” “I thought it crashed,” or “I couldn’t click the Like button while it was processing.” These observations are exactly what the upcoming vocabulary will explain.\n\n\n\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students didn't notice a clear difference during Level 1, have them run the demo again before discussing. Prompt them to click the Like button first, then the Compress Video button, and pay attention to whether the app responds while the task is running.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "58baaa9e-ab31-4a80-a278-9dcb58b67176",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71d64eb1-7006-4f12-98f7-da133e1bc727",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Have students get out their Designing Reliable Apps with AI and APIs Unit Guide and turn to Lesson 2.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define synchronous code and connect it directly to the Frozen App demo. Point out that the Compress Video button is an example of synchronous behavior — the app waited for that task to fully finish before doing anything else. Direct students to fill in the synchronous code definition in their Unit Guide.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The timeline diagram is key here. Walk students through it left to right — Task 1 must fully finish before Task 2 starts, and Task 2 must fully finish before Task 3 starts. Ask students: \"How does this match what you saw in the app?\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71d64eb1-7006-4f12-98f7-da133e1bc727",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3306926c-7752-4883-8dc9-dbeaba787147",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define asynchronous code and connect it to both the Restaurant Analogy and the Frozen App demo. Point out that the Like button is an example of asynchronous behavior — the app started the task and kept running without waiting. Remind students of the chef: starting the water boiling and chopping vegetables at the same time is exactly what asynchronous code does. Direct students to fill in the asynchronous code definition in their Unit Guide.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Walk through the diagram and contrast it directly with the synchronous timeline from the previous slide. Ask students: \"What's different about how these tasks run?\" Listen for students noticing that tasks overlap rather than wait. This is also a good moment to address a common misconception — asynchronous doesn't mean tasks happen simultaneously in the same instant; it means one task doesn't have to fully finish before another can start.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3306926c-7752-4883-8dc9-dbeaba787147",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b382f99a-cbf2-4735-b4fc-1745f5c13706",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation in order to explain that synchronous code waits for each line to finish before moving on, while asynchronous code can start a task for later and keep running the next lines.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to demonstrate how setTimeout() works. Point out that lines 1–3 hand cookEggs() off to run after three seconds, while lines 5–6 run immediately. Connect this back to the chef analogy — setTimeout() is like putting water on to boil and walking away to chop vegetables. Direct students to complete the setTimeout() annotation in their Unit Guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may wonder why makeToast() and pourJuice() run before cookEggs() even though they appear lower in the code. Clarify that setTimeout() schedules cookEggs() to run later — JavaScript doesn't wait for the timer, it keeps reading and running the next lines immediately. This is the core mechanic behind asynchronous code and worth pausing on before moving to the platform levels.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b382f99a-cbf2-4735-b4fc-1745f5c13706",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b2a3a269-4773-44a7-934d-3e2eb361a5f9",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply their understanding of synchronous and asynchronous execution by predicting and investigating code behavior across three choice levels, then demonstrate understanding in a written CFU response.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Why Apps Wait activity guide.\n\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 2 to complete all of the choice levels in Level 2 and the Check for Understanding in Level 3.\n- **Level 2a:** Students predict how blocking code freezes the interface while tasks complete one at a time.\n- **Level 2b:** Students predict the execution order of multiple kitchen tasks running on different timers.\n- **Level 2c:** Students predict the outcome when the chef tries to serve food before the cooking timer finishes.\n- **Level 3:** Students demonstrate their understanding of asynchronous code by explaining why the server used the value before the burger finished cooking.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Sync vs Async Kitchen",
        "progression_name": "Synchronous vs. Asynchronous ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 2c and Level 3 assess students' ability to define asynchronous programming and identify where things can go wrong in an async timeline (Objectives 1 and 3). For Level 2c, look for students recognizing that data used before it's ready produces an error. For Level 3, look for responses that explain the timing mismatch in their own words rather than restating the definition.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 8 is a critical \"Aha!\" moment. Ensure students understand that trying to use data before it has \"cooked\" results in an error."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b2a3a269-4773-44a7-934d-3e2eb361a5f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a7e30ed-2f79-46dc-bda1-48f73ca4e101",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a7e30ed-2f79-46dc-bda1-48f73ca4e101",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3087e702-366d-466a-85dd-8f5da26619bf",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**  Students consolidate their understanding of synchronous and asynchronous execution by visually mapping task timing from code they just investigated and comparing results with a partner.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Tracing the Timeline"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3087e702-366d-466a-85dd-8f5da26619bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47a85e36-df75-428a-a0b4-0d7b107b86a6",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's map out exactly what happened in each kitchen using a timeline graph.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47a85e36-df75-428a-a0b4-0d7b107b86a6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d97655d8-0bbd-4daf-9038-29870f7fb773",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs with their neighbor. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct Partner A to look back at Level 2a and draw a timeline graph of the code. Partner B looks back at Level 2b or 2c and draws a timeline graph of their code. Remind students the diagrams on the slide show what sync and async timelines look like as a reference. When both partners finish, they compare their timelines and discuss what looks different.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are labeling both tasks and wait times in their timelines, not just drawing the shapes. Listen for partners noticing that the sync timeline runs tasks one after another while the async timeline shows tasks overlapping. When time is up, bring the class together and transition to the Wrap Up.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Providing different colored pencils or pens helps make parallel execution visible — suggest students use one color per task so overlapping timelines are easy to read. If Partner B chose Level 2b, encourage them to also look at Level 2c and note how the timeline changes when the chef tries to serve food before the cooking timer finishes.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d97655d8-0bbd-4daf-9038-29870f7fb773",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6b4db25f-625c-4a4c-ae91-d7eefc50c942"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "69363d90-2344-4b67-a474-b4282b5f5a6d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to answer the question using something specific from the lesson — the Frozen App demo, the chef analogy, or the setTimeout() code. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. Listen for students connecting the idea that JavaScript keeps running the next lines while a delayed task waits in the background — that's why the code runs before the data arrives.\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is short, skip the partner talk and go straight to whole-class share-out. Prioritize getting at least one strong response to the primary question before moving to the secondary — the technical understanding should land before the UX reflection.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "69363d90-2344-4b67-a474-b4282b5f5a6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6be212df-5fa7-4638-b747-3b1fbe4e17f4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16799d3e-9913-4de1-932f-aa744a4d7f31",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16799d3e-9913-4de1-932f-aa744a4d7f31",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6be212df-5fa7-4638-b747-3b1fbe4e17f4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0d358404-ae85-484f-bd04-71e0566cd200",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use a real-world analogy to understand the states of an asynchronous request.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** You ordered a product and check its delivery status. What would each of these mean for your package? (Pending, Fulfilled, Rejected)\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students visualize the waiting period and possible outcomes of a delivery. Listen for students connecting each state to a clear outcome — this sets the stage for the concept of a Promise.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle, prompt them: Pending is the package still on the truck. Fulfilled is a successful delivery. Rejected is a \"Package Lost\" notification.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0d358404-ae85-484f-bd04-71e0566cd200",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaaae204-6f74-4a52-8836-c6b0cdb6b4e3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da0375ad-8e61-40c9-9d4c-87df744356aa",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da0375ad-8e61-40c9-9d4c-87df744356aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaaae204-6f74-4a52-8836-c6b0cdb6b4e3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f868dd15-b705-4b57-a613-50dcb9655d70",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explicitly connect the student definitions to the JavaScript Promise language: Pending, Fulfilled, and Rejected."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f868dd15-b705-4b57-a613-50dcb9655d70",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaaae204-6f74-4a52-8836-c6b0cdb6b4e3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "98357e9b-6564-4c2c-b74b-e0a79ade2b17",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In JavaScript, when we fetch data, we get back a Promise — a placeholder for a result that isn't ready yet. Just like a package, it starts Pending, and ends in exactly one state: Fulfilled if it succeeds, or Rejected if something goes wrong.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "98357e9b-6564-4c2c-b74b-e0a79ade2b17",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaaae204-6f74-4a52-8836-c6b0cdb6b4e3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a7a212a-ae56-473c-a5d9-951f52f50f93",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a7a212a-ae56-473c-a5d9-951f52f50f93",
        "lesson_activity.key": "eaaae204-6f74-4a52-8836-c6b0cdb6b4e3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4a08966c-ae5f-4c3a-9812-bb5e5a711f09",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explore a real Promise chain and build the vocabulary and conceptual understanding needed to write one on their own.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:**  Have students get out their Designing Reliable Apps with AI and APIs Unit Guide and turn to Lesson 3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students time to annotate the Promise chain in their Unit Guide. Have them mark the parts they think send a request, handle arriving data, and handle something going wrong. Then click through the animation to reveal the highlighted sections.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  Listen for students connecting fetch(url) to sending a request, the .then() blocks to handling data, and .catch() to handling failure. \n\n\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Explore & Annotate a Promise Chain ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame this as a low-stakes exploration before students begin. Let them know they won't know all the answers yet — that's the point. Encouraging guessing and pattern-noticing here is more valuable than accuracy.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4a08966c-ae5f-4c3a-9812-bb5e5a711f09",
        "lesson_activity.key": "369c103d-02de-41ce-aa19-a04f5d1015a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c131434c-3731-475c-93d6-cd5b08c40d65",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\nWhat did you notice while annotating the Promise chain?\nWhat do you wonder about how the code moves from one step to the next?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:**  Surface students' initial observations about the Promise chain's structure before vocabulary is introduced. Students do not need to define Promise, .then(), or .catch() yet. Listen for students noticing that the code starts by asking for data, steps through handling the result, and has a final part for when something goes wrong. These observations become the foundation for the vocabulary slides that follow.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students only noticed surface-level details like line count or punctuation, prompt further: \"Which part do you think starts the request?\" or \"Which part looks like it handles success or failure?\" This redirects attention to the behavior of the chain without formally defining terms yet.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c131434c-3731-475c-93d6-cd5b08c40d65",
        "lesson_activity.key": "369c103d-02de-41ce-aa19-a04f5d1015a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bc0f93a4-02a9-4fc9-b7d2-baf7f9d701a8",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *Promise*. Connect the definition back to what students just annotated — the fetch(url) line they identified as \"sending a request\" is the moment a Promise is created.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the tracking number analogy: you don't have the package yet, but you have a guarantee that it either arrives or you get notified if the delivery fails. The mail order receipt visual on the slide reinforces this — note the \"TRACKING: [PENDING]\" status.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bc0f93a4-02a9-4fc9-b7d2-baf7f9d701a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "369c103d-02de-41ce-aa19-a04f5d1015a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f48d5708-3349-4388-81e4-82454fbcf250",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's trace what actually happens when fetch() runs — using the same Pending, Fulfilled, and Rejected states from the warm up, now mapped to real code.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f48d5708-3349-4388-81e4-82454fbcf250",
        "lesson_activity.key": "369c103d-02de-41ce-aa19-a04f5d1015a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "be9e6779-86a1-4f8c-84fd-3d9bcd66e709",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to walk students through each stage of a Promise. Have students add short annotations to the diagram in their Unit Guide as each stage is revealed.\n* **Fetch Request:** This is the moment we call fetch(). The request is sent to the server. fetch() does not wait for the answer. It immediately gives us a Promise object.\n* **Pending:** As soon as the request is sent, the Promise is in the Pending state. Pending means the request is still in progress. We don’t have data yet. We don’t have an error yet. We are waiting.\n* **Fulfilled:** If the server responds successfully, the Promise becomes Fulfilled. That usually means we received a successful HTTP response, like 200 OK, and we now have data to work with. The Promise has resolved successfully.\n* **Rejected:** If something goes wrong — maybe the server crashes, the network fails, or the request can’t complete — the Promise becomes Rejected. Instead of data, we receive an error.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Don't rush through the animation. Give students 15–20 seconds after each stage appears to write their annotation before clicking to the next. If students ask what HTTP 200 or HTTP 500 means, a brief answer is fine — \"200 means the server said yes, 500 means something broke on the server's end\" — but hold the deeper explanation for a later lesson.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "be9e6779-86a1-4f8c-84fd-3d9bcd66e709",
        "lesson_activity.key": "369c103d-02de-41ce-aa19-a04f5d1015a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "961f74f3-ad03-4a24-8249-fa5c23f96310",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n* *If the Promise is Fulfilled and we finally have the data, what do we want our app to do next?*  \n* *If we don’t handle the Rejected state, what might happen in our app?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect Promise outcomes to app behavior — what to display on success, and what happens if errors go unhandled. Listen for students recognizing that the two outcomes need separate code paths. This sets up the need for .then() and .catch() on the next slides.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the then() method. Connect it directly to the Fulfilled path students identified in the diagram — .then() is how we write the code that runs when the Promise succeeds. Have students add .then() to their Fulfilled state annotation in the Unit Guide diagram before moving to the next slide.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the catch() method. Connect it to the Rejected path in the diagram — .catch() is how we write the code that runs when the Promise fails. Have students add .catch() to their Rejected state annotation in the Unit Guide diagram.\n\n\n\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Promise",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students sometimes think .catch() only handles internet or network failures. Clarify that it catches any rejection in the Promise chain — including bad URLs, server errors, or anything that causes the Promise to fail. Reinforce that without .catch(), a rejected Promise can cause the app to silently break with no feedback to the user.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "961f74f3-ad03-4a24-8249-fa5c23f96310",
        "lesson_activity.key": "369c103d-02de-41ce-aa19-a04f5d1015a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4c76c1d5-1487-4ab1-a787-3bf0bd0b96cc",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to show what each parameter holds in the Promise chain. Explain that response holds the server’s reply, data holds the usable JSON data after response.json(), and err holds information about what failed if the request is rejected. Have students label the parameters in their Unit Guide as each annotation is revealed.\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: .then() and .catch()",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students often think response, data, and err are required keywords. Clarify that these are parameter names — they could be named anything, but these names are conventional and descriptive. Using meaningful names here makes the code easier to read and debug."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4c76c1d5-1487-4ab1-a787-3bf0bd0b96cc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "369c103d-02de-41ce-aa19-a04f5d1015a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0968172b-96ce-48f2-b630-49edb6f6f19f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice and wonder about the number of .then() and .catch() calls in the code below?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that a Promise chain can have multiple .then() calls chained together, each passing its result to the next, while a single .catch() at the end handles any failure in the chain. Listen for students connecting this pattern to the Fulfilled and Rejected paths from the diagram.\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may wonder why there's only one .catch() for two .then() calls. Let them sit with that question for now — the Tracing the Promise Timeline slide addresses it. If students ask directly, a brief answer is fine: \"One .catch() at the end catches any error from anywhere in the chain.\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0968172b-96ce-48f2-b630-49edb6f6f19f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "369c103d-02de-41ce-aa19-a04f5d1015a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ab17637-cefa-4cfa-b82f-7e7ed7c9ecc3",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:**  Click through the animated slide to walk students through the timeline of an asynchronous request. Have students add annotations to the timeline in their Unit Guide as each step is revealed."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ab17637-cefa-4cfa-b82f-7e7ed7c9ecc3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "369c103d-02de-41ce-aa19-a04f5d1015a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b9b50c9-d5e1-4128-8bd2-fbd16aa67ed1",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Code doesn't run top-to-bottom instantly here — there's a waiting period where the Promise sits until the server responds.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that .catch() is a safety net. If the internet goes down or the URL is wrong, .catch() keeps the app from silently breaking with no feedback to the user.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "These Unit Guide annotations can serve as a formative check on Lesson Objective 2 — examining the returned Promise and response object. Look for students accurately labeling each step of the chain before they move to the levels.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b9b50c9-d5e1-4128-8bd2-fbd16aa67ed1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "369c103d-02de-41ce-aa19-a04f5d1015a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a6bccdb-eeba-4848-b644-68c125a42387",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:**Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 1 to complete Levels 1-3. \n* **Level 1:** Students can review the difference between the first and second .then() methods\n* **Level 2:** Students write their own Promise chain. \n* **Level 3:** Students demonstrate their understanding of .then() and .catch() in a promise chain. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** For students who need additional support, direct them to review Level 1 and use what they find there to complete or strengthen their Promise States and Methods annotations in their Unit Guide before continuing to Level 2. When students are on Level 2, ensure they are putting their DOM update code inside the second .then(). If they put it outside, it will run before the data exists.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Build a Promise Chain ",
        "progression_name": "Build a Promise Chain",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For Level 2, suggest students temporarily break their URL (e.g., change 'api' to 'apix') to test if their .catch() block actually works.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 3 is a formative assessment of Lesson Objectives 1 and 2 — writing a fetch call and examining the returned Promise. Look for students correctly chaining .then() for success handling and .catch() for error handling.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a6bccdb-eeba-4848-b644-68c125a42387",
        "lesson_activity.key": "369c103d-02de-41ce-aa19-a04f5d1015a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "300ec432-be52-46b1-a85b-d1205df310ac",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answer using a specific example from the lesson — the Promise chain they built, a state they annotated, or how .then() and .catch() handled their data. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share with the class. Listen for students connecting fetch() creating a Promise, the Pending state while waiting, .then() handling the Fulfilled response, and .catch() handling any failure.\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is short, skip the partner talk and do a quick whole-class share-out instead — collect two or three responses that together cover the full fetch lifecycle: the request being sent, the waiting period, and both possible outcomes. This ensures the complete picture is named before students leave.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "300ec432-be52-46b1-a85b-d1205df310ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7a7539b8-0f2a-422d-b715-f8060f169df7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72eb7a8d-7f0f-460d-8525-22e295b4b23c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72eb7a8d-7f0f-460d-8525-22e295b4b23c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7a7539b8-0f2a-422d-b715-f8060f169df7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f1600140-1ecd-4f00-b324-043db4f33ebd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students connect the concept of \"waiting\" to real-world tasks to understand when await is necessary in code.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Think about tasks you complete in your morning routine. What can you do at the same time? What has to wait for something else before it can happen?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify that some tasks are independent (like checking your phone while getting dressed) while others have dependencies (you can't get on the bus until it arrives). Guide students toward recognizing that dependencies require waiting — this mirrors how code must sometimes pause for a server response before continuing."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f1600140-1ecd-4f00-b324-043db4f33ebd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ba818faf-e4fc-4aa2-9287-fbc944987d7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f731a173-4b51-4f0d-9e7c-767d24e12ef6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice that some of your tasks can happen at the same time. They don’t depend on anything else. But other tasks can’t start until something else finishes. You can’t get on the bus until it arrives. You can’t eat toast until it’s done toasting. In programming, we run into the same situation. Some code can run right away. But sometimes, our program has to wait for something, like data from a server, before it can continue.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f731a173-4b51-4f0d-9e7c-767d24e12ef6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ba818faf-e4fc-4aa2-9287-fbc944987d7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09f6ee70-8236-415e-9e9e-912b0b1d9a4b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09f6ee70-8236-415e-9e9e-912b0b1d9a4b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ba818faf-e4fc-4aa2-9287-fbc944987d7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "91126a0e-e462-4eec-a138-94aa2e6dd2d3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students connect the concept of synchronous vs. asynchronous code to real-world waiting, then apply that thinking by writing pseudocode for their morning routine and identifying which steps require waiting.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Provide each pair a copy of the Making Steps Clear Activity Guide.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students of the difference between synchronous and asynchronous code. Connect it back to the warm up discussion — some morning routine tasks could happen at the same time, while others had to wait. Point out that this is exactly what's happening in code: asynchronous code keeps moving while waiting tasks run in the background.\n\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Morning Routine",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use the analogy of ordering food. Synchronous is standing at the counter until your food is ready. Asynchronous is taking a buzzer to your table so you can keep talking while you wait. This sets up the pseudocode format students will use in their activity guide.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "91126a0e-e462-4eec-a138-94aa2e6dd2d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ae072eb5-656a-482a-b1e3-2bab134b454a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students work with their partner to complete the morning routine pseudocode on page one of their activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who mark every step with await. Remind them that await is only for things outside their control, like a toaster or a bus. Ask: \"Can you brush your teeth while the toast is cooking?\"\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use the activity guide to informally assess whether students can identify external dependencies versus internal actions — this maps to the objective: Explain what async does to a function and what await does to a Promise. Look for accurate use of await only on steps that require waiting for something outside the student's control."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ae072eb5-656a-482a-b1e3-2bab134b454a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f83d4782-4bec-44c1-80a8-dd89c144deb7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**  Students recognize the readability benefits of async/await by directly comparing it to .then() syntax and articulating why a linear, top-to-bottom structure is easier to read and debug.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to page two of their activity guide. Give them a few minutes to review both programs before discussing as a class.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Comparing Promise Chains ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Let students look at the two programs before saying anything about them. The goal is for students to notice differences themselves first — this sets up the Discuss slide with more student-generated observations rather than teacher-delivered conclusions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f83d4782-4bec-44c1-80a8-dd89c144deb7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c0708f16-cf20-46d9-a6eb-2ef18476d0ed",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n* *Which version would you rather debug?*\n* *Why does top-to-bottom flow help?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that async/await is easier to read because it follows a linear, top-to-bottom flow — the same way most code is written and read. If students focus only on aesthetics (\"it looks cleaner\"), push further: \"What specifically makes it easier to track what's happening at each step?\"\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c0708f16-cf20-46d9-a6eb-2ef18476d0ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5cb8aa05-92de-40ee-b3ef-036eb4e8f964",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use the Unit Guide to build understanding of the async and await keywords through annotated code examples, connecting the vocabulary to what they explored in the morning routine activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Have students get out their Designing Reliable Apps with AI and APIs Unit Guide and turn to Lesson 4.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "async/await Keywords "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5cb8aa05-92de-40ee-b3ef-036eb4e8f964",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7c550772-26ec-4ec2-a740-32682cd8f318",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the async keyword. Have students add a description in their own words to the async keyword in the code annotation on their Unit Guide.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are struggling, have them think of async as a label on a box — it tells JavaScript \"inside this box, we're going to be doing some waiting.\" Point out that async appears in the code example right before the arrow function — that placement is intentional and students will see it consistently every time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7c550772-26ec-4ec2-a740-32682cd8f318",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ad6b20b-3b5d-4619-af72-86b8f0476128",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ad6b20b-3b5d-4619-af72-86b8f0476128",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c2428fec-1c5e-4188-93f6-3c5f3f20c26a",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the await keyword. Have students add a description in their own words to the await keyword in the code annotation on their Unit Guide.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that await can ONLY be used inside an async function — they are a pair. A useful way to remember it: you can't wait for something if you haven't told the function it's allowed to wait in the first place."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c2428fec-1c5e-4188-93f6-3c5f3f20c26a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48247bc1-e5aa-4e13-8a36-bfc4439ca95f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48247bc1-e5aa-4e13-8a36-bfc4439ca95f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bab0123c-9b40-4522-bd48-d73a973da08e",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the async/await pattern using the annotated code. Have students complete the annotation for the async/await Pattern in their Unit Guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Contrast this with the .then() syntax from the previous lesson. Point out how const response = and const data = sit directly on the left side of each await call — variables are assigned in a single readable line rather than nested inside a .then() callback.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bab0123c-9b40-4522-bd48-d73a973da08e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb5d0eda-6059-4edd-a752-63dee58ca59f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb5d0eda-6059-4edd-a752-63dee58ca59f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3cbf6366-69f6-4538-9de4-b9eb62db689b",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply async/await refactoring skills independently and with AI assistance, then reflect on when AI is useful and why human oversight of AI-generated code matters.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- Level 1: Students use an AI tutor to write refactored code for their random joke API.\n- Level 2: Students manually refactor the code and decide which version (AI or human) to use and justify their choice.\n- Level 3: Students demonstrate their understanding of `async` and `await` by labeling how a `.then()` chain would be refactored and explaining what each `await` does.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask students: 'Did the AI use await correctly? Did it keep the variables named the same way you did?' Encourage them to find one difference between their code and the AI's code.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Human vs AI Refactoring",
        "progression_name": "Refactoring with AI",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 2 and 3 to informally assess student progress toward two objectives: Convert a basic fetch call to use async and await — look for correct placement of async on the function and await before fetch() and response.json(); and Explain what async does to a function and what await does to a Promise — look for accurate labels and explanations in Level 3, not just correct syntax.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3cbf6366-69f6-4538-9de4-b9eb62db689b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "05a39036-3056-488e-9e24-c64cf4846625",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *When is it better to use AI?*\n- *Why check AI code for errors?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that AI can speed up repetitive tasks like refactoring, but humans need to verify the output — checking that logic, variable names, and execution order are correct. Guide students toward the idea that the human role isn't to avoid AI, but to stay in control of it. If students have encountered the term \"Human-in-the-Loop,\" this is a natural place to connect it.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "05a39036-3056-488e-9e24-c64cf4846625",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a0084df-cd5f-4560-8cbc-9b6b8d3a036e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "19ab9a3a-f106-454c-a959-9e2acff8f7c9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answer using a specific example from the lesson — the async/await pattern they annotated, the refactored code they wrote, or a comparison they made between .then() and async/await. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. Listen for students connecting async/await syntax to the idea of writing asynchronous code that reads like normal, step-by-step code.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19ab9a3a-f106-454c-a959-9e2acff8f7c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c56fef8f-5b6a-41ba-bf2f-391a11d63eec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf24b0a9-9a97-4c94-a6c8-2c3fb749f2b1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf24b0a9-9a97-4c94-a6c8-2c3fb749f2b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c56fef8f-5b6a-41ba-bf2f-391a11d63eec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "10dc01f9-1d25-4124-8175-cc4afe63271f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Engage students with the idea that UI elements come from specific pieces of JSON data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the UI card and raw JSON side by side. Give students a moment to look at both quietly before asking anything.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If you were building this app, which 3 pieces of information would you want from this data? What looks unuseful?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students start to see that not all data from an API is equally important. Guide them toward identifying a small set of key fields that directly match what they see on the UI card (for example, name, a description, one key stat) and labeling the rest as “nice to have” or “extra.”\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "10dc01f9-1d25-4124-8175-cc4afe63271f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f39c74c-347b-438d-98ce-87e06e0d7c90"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dbea9595-718f-4e1e-a2a8-924cb4084625",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we will learn how to read this \"strange\" JSON text as organized data and turn it into something we can loop over and display in our apps.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dbea9595-718f-4e1e-a2a8-924cb4084625",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f39c74c-347b-438d-98ce-87e06e0d7c90"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37771bb8-f52b-4786-9cf7-12f4c5894796",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37771bb8-f52b-4786-9cf7-12f4c5894796",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f39c74c-347b-438d-98ce-87e06e0d7c90"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c08214b8-181f-46ff-a0ae-066f4e085bab",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explore the structure of a real JSON response by identifying visual patterns — brackets, braces, nested sections, and value types — before vocabulary is formally introduced. This builds readiness for the JSON Structure slide that follows.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 1 to work in Sketch Lab. Students make their best guesses about the structure of the JSON by highlighting and labeling patterns they notice, including brackets, braces, nested sections, and different kinds of values. They may not know every answer yet — the goal is to look closely and predict how the data is organized before the structure is formally explained.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students using visual clues in the JSON, such as [], {}, quotation marks, numbers, and repeated key:value patterns. Ask questions like: \n* What symbols seem to group the whole dataset? \n* Where does one pet entry seem to begin and end? \n* What looks like it is inside something else? \n\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Annotate a JSON ",
        "progression_name": "Seeing the Shape of the JSON",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students feel overwhelmed by the size of the JSON, encourage them to focus on one symbol or pattern at a time rather than trying to understand the whole thing at once. The :@computed_region fields near the bottom may confuse students — let them know it's okay to notice those look different and set them aside for now.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c08214b8-181f-46ff-a0ae-066f4e085bab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "592b4703-cf99-4e80-9361-d07c0d28f8e9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "32fba4aa-ff6b-4738-afab-196a0c6d3431",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the annotated JSON and walk through each color-coded pattern. Connect what students just labeled in Level 1 to the correct terms: the outer [ ] is the array, each { } is an object, text and number values are strings and numbers, and location and image are nested objects.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This slide uses a simplified version of the Level 1 JSON — fields like at_aac, intake_date, color, and the :@computed_region entries have been removed to make the four structural patterns easier to see. Give students a heads-up so they aren't thrown off when comparing this to what they annotated. You might say: \"This is the same JSON, cleaned up so the patterns stand out.\" Point students to the JSON Structure section of their unit guide — let them know this same annotated JSON is there as a reference as they work through the remaining levels.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "32fba4aa-ff6b-4738-afab-196a0c6d3431",
        "lesson_activity.key": "592b4703-cf99-4e80-9361-d07c0d28f8e9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5231074d-9018-46f2-ae64-fcc0e8177241",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Have students get out their Designing Reliable Apps with AI and APIs Unit Guide and turn to Lesson 5.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5231074d-9018-46f2-ae64-fcc0e8177241",
        "lesson_activity.key": "592b4703-cf99-4e80-9361-d07c0d28f8e9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a2744890-20d8-49e9-aa89-ca9a03687a6b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When you can see where things live inside the JSON, you can follow a path to grab exactly the value you need. The next step is learning the pattern we use to write those paths.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a2744890-20d8-49e9-aa89-ca9a03687a6b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "592b4703-cf99-4e80-9361-d07c0d28f8e9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "17918a40-7c3b-40ff-af14-42e6fb97c8d6",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *dot notation*. Point out the code example — show how data[0] gets the first item in the array and .looks_like accesses that key on the object. Direct students to fill in the dot notation definition in their unit guide.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to show how an object in the array is accessed and how dot notation is used to access a key on the object and chain into a nested object.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define .map(). Click through the animation to show how .map() processes one item at a time, builds a new object using return, and pulls values from each item using dot notation. Point out that the dot notation students just learned is being used here inside the .map() callback to access values from each pet object.\n\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Connect dot notation to the new idea of transforming arrays. Walk through the before/after JSON to show how .map() selects only the fields needed, renames keys to be more UI-friendly, and can pull values out of nested objects to flatten the structure.\n",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Dot Notation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "17918a40-7c3b-40ff-af14-42e6fb97c8d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "592b4703-cf99-4e80-9361-d07c0d28f8e9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f44dde9e-4c67-406e-98e2-36f86dd93e7e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply their understanding of dot notation and .map() by designing a cleaned object from the pet shelter JSON, choosing UI-relevant fields, writing dot-notation paths, and explaining their choices — building toward the CFU at Level 4.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Levels 2-4.\n* **Level 2:** Students use the JSON snippet to write dot-notation paths.\n* **Level 3:** Students imagine an info card. Choose three properties, give each a clear key name in the clean object, match each to a dot-notation path in the raw JSON, and explain why it is useful for the UI.\n* **Level 4:** Students demonstrate their understanding of dot notation and .map() by accessing a value in an array and explaining how to create a cleaner array of objects.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who choose UI-relevant fields and pair them with correct dot-notation paths. Prompt with: What will each item in your new array look like? How will this help you loop and render cards later?\n\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Design a Mapped Object",
        "progression_name": "Design a Mapped Object",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students keep guessing paths, have them trace with their finger from the top-level object down to the value, saying each key out loud as before turning it into code..\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 4 responses to check if students can connect JSON structure, dot notation, and UI needs. Strong responses pick appropriate fields, write valid dot-notation paths, and give a clear reason each field helps the UI. Offer quick feedback or conferencing with students who only copy raw field names without thinking about the UI.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f44dde9e-4c67-406e-98e2-36f86dd93e7e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "592b4703-cf99-4e80-9361-d07c0d28f8e9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c17d7d7-7203-4c86-af17-044af9caee93",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answer using a specific example from the lesson — the JSON they annotated, a dot-notation path they wrote, or a .map() transformation they designed. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share with the class. Listen for students connecting JSON.parse() turning text into a JavaScript array, dot notation accessing specific values, and .map() transforming the array into a cleaner structure for the UI.\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is short, skip the partner talk and do a quick whole-class share-out instead — collect two or three responses that together cover the full answer: parsing the JSON, accessing values with dot notation, and transforming the array with .map(). This ensures the complete picture is named before students leave.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c17d7d7-7203-4c86-af17-044af9caee93",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0b8b4beb-d1cf-4da3-b454-fb2145551f91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8fca0873-e39c-4aa4-b15c-de77da307bd4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8fca0873-e39c-4aa4-b15c-de77da307bd4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0b8b4beb-d1cf-4da3-b454-fb2145551f91"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dbf9c71a-61e9-4f35-8129-22dc1001e9a7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** StStudents see that the same data can be visualized in multiple ways, setting up the lesson's exploration of data-driven content and layout decisions.\nudents see that the same data can be visualized in multiple ways."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dbf9c71a-61e9-4f35-8129-22dc1001e9a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dae705e6-5ea5-4230-8275-32d1e1633382"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8477bec0-cf55-42bc-bfa3-09e260e9ad45",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *When is it more helpful for a user to see the data as a card layout?*\n- *When is it more helpful to see it in a list view?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that layout choices depend on user needs. Listen for students connecting card layouts to browsing or image-heavy content, and list views to scanning or comparing data quickly. Both are correct — the goal is for students to see that the same data can serve different purposes depending on how it's displayed.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8477bec0-cf55-42bc-bfa3-09e260e9ad45",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dae705e6-5ea5-4230-8275-32d1e1633382"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3b5ffa5-6626-413c-9f9c-b3cbf706a36b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:**  Students explore the relationship between raw JSON data and a finished card layout, then connect that exploration to the key concepts of data-driven content and parsing JSON.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:**  Click through the animated slide to display the prompts:\n* *What do you notice about the JSON data? What information is there?*\n* *What would you need to understand about the data before you could build the card?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for students noticing specific fields in the JSON (track, artist, image_url, etc.) and connecting them to what appears on the card. Guide students toward recognizing that you need to understand what the data contains — its fields, types, and structure — before you can design with it. Avoid introducing \"parsing\" or \"JSON\" as defined terms here; those land on the next slides.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "From JSON to Design"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3b5ffa5-6626-413c-9f9c-b3cbf706a36b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "92865c38-4858-4ded-9077-ea8cf1675325"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "964ec84a-a043-4c3e-a800-96d188911c1a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Have students get out their copy of the Designing Reliable Apps with AI and APIs Unit Guide and turn to Lesson 6.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "964ec84a-a043-4c3e-a800-96d188911c1a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "92865c38-4858-4ded-9077-ea8cf1675325"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "734c8a6b-6967-4284-887f-abebfd0fc166",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *data-driven content*. Direct students to record it in their unit guide. Connect this definition back to the discussion students just had — they were already thinking about data-driven content when they considered what a developer needs to know before building a card from JSON. \"Dynamically generated\" may be unfamiliar; clarify that it means the page content is built from data automatically, so if the data changes, the page updates too — without anyone rewriting the HTML by hand.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the term Parsing JSON and its definition. Direct students to record it in their unit guide. Help students understand that parsing is the translation step between raw data and usable code — computers read JSON strings the same way we read sentences, finding the structure to extract meaning. If students wonder why they can't just use the raw string directly in HTML, use this as a quick comprehension check: a raw string is just text; parsing turns it into a JavaScript object your code can actually work with.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "734c8a6b-6967-4284-887f-abebfd0fc166",
        "lesson_activity.key": "92865c38-4858-4ded-9077-ea8cf1675325"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "89c2842f-a52f-4095-a823-e54e725dc5d5",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply their understanding of JSON and data-driven content by building a web app that renders cards from an array of objects, debugging issues caused by inconsistent JSON structure, and demonstrating their understanding in a CFU.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 1 to complete Levels 1-4. \n* **Level 1:** Students design a visual mockup of a content card or playlist view in Sketch Lab.\n* **Level 2:** Students build a web app to display their music playlist or card view.\n* **Level 3:** Students adjust their loop logic and add safeguards to handle the new JSON structure.\n* **Level 4:** Students answer the Check for Understanding question.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students struggling with loop syntax and make sure they use the correct variable names inside the loop. Remind students that key names in the JSON must match exactly what they use in their code, including case sensitivity — this is a common source of bugs where data simply doesn't show up. Ensure students are choosing keys that make sense for their app (for example, a card view needs the image URL).\n",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Building from the Blueprint",
        "progression_name": "Building from the Blueprint",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that the key name in the JSON must match exactly what they call in their code, including case sensitivity, or the data won't show up.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 3 and 4 to assess student understanding of the lesson objectives. In Level 3, check whether students have successfully updated their code to handle the new JSON structure. In Level 4, review student responses to assess their ability to loop through data and generate UI cards from an array of objects.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "89c2842f-a52f-4095-a823-e54e725dc5d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "92865c38-4858-4ded-9077-ea8cf1675325"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d96c0f2-4087-48e8-bccd-2535b63732ff",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *When you make an API call and get back a JSON response, how can you design your code so it still works if a property is different than expected?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that they can write code defensively — checking whether a property exists before using it, providing fallback values for missing data, and avoiding hard-coded property names without validation. Listen for students connecting this to what they experienced in Level 3, where the JSON structure changed and their code broke.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d96c0f2-4087-48e8-bccd-2535b63732ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "92865c38-4858-4ded-9077-ea8cf1675325"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d0c1ea46-62c9-4233-b59e-15db098b6eb1",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Sometimes the data we get from a server isn't perfect. It might be messy, in the wrong format, or missing entirely. Your job as a developer is to make the app look good anyway!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d0c1ea46-62c9-4233-b59e-15db098b6eb1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "92865c38-4858-4ded-9077-ea8cf1675325"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c27e93e-1cf3-4cca-b2ed-c3e7b82b067f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answer using a specific example from the lesson — their loop logic, a JSON field they accessed, or a card they built. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. Listen for students connecting looping through an array to the cards that appeared on the page, and for students who can name the relationship between a JSON field and what showed up in their UI.\n\n\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c27e93e-1cf3-4cca-b2ed-c3e7b82b067f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5dc4db5a-8c4f-4709-a0fe-20ae86a18871"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c6c2661-4208-4c1e-9b74-ed4ab90affb7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c6c2661-4208-4c1e-9b74-ed4ab90affb7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5dc4db5a-8c4f-4709-a0fe-20ae86a18871"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "857b6ff2-f672-4be2-b0e2-9767c241fe12",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students observe how different apps handle system failures and consider the impact on user experience."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "857b6ff2-f672-4be2-b0e2-9767c241fe12",
        "lesson_activity.key": "27178ab6-e9ad-4ff7-a90e-e625385fd117"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fbc73759-4912-4b0a-b434-fd8815742803",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We have all seen screens like these. Whether it is a “404” page, a loading spinner that never ends, or a message that says something went wrong, these screens are a developer’s way of handling a problem. Today, we are going to think about the user experience when a system failure occurs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fbc73759-4912-4b0a-b434-fd8815742803",
        "lesson_activity.key": "27178ab6-e9ad-4ff7-a90e-e625385fd117"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "20caf013-f88c-4af4-92ed-a5f27d695e7b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How helpful is the error message?*\n- *How does the page make you feel ?*\n- *What is one improvement that you would make?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice whether the page feels friendly, clear, and helpful. Push students to identify whether the user knows what happened and what to do next.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to think about the “vibe” of the page. Is it playful and apologetic, or cold and technical? This sets the stage for understanding human-centered reliability.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "20caf013-f88c-4af4-92ed-a5f27d695e7b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "27178ab6-e9ad-4ff7-a90e-e625385fd117"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3940b3b5-0473-4b2f-95ad-9a7049781a88",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3940b3b5-0473-4b2f-95ad-9a7049781a88",
        "lesson_activity.key": "27178ab6-e9ad-4ff7-a90e-e625385fd117"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b0f3e50b-1b4c-4807-ac60-2845ced84f43",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b0f3e50b-1b4c-4807-ac60-2845ced84f43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "27178ab6-e9ad-4ff7-a90e-e625385fd117"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e14a5cd3-e05a-4db2-b34b-f724d42d9574",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn what Reliability Engineers do and connect the role to trustworthy app experiences.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Reliability Engineer career.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Frame the student task. For this lesson, students will act as a Reliability Engineer for a concert ticketing application.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Reliability Engineer"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e14a5cd3-e05a-4db2-b34b-f724d42d9574",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83f9cc83-409d-480c-8787-9bc6c026de13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a3666d4-8076-4e58-881b-97d81f707691",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you will act as Reliability Engineers for a concert ticketing application. Imagine users are trying to buy tickets, choose seats, or view their account, but something in the system breaks. Your job has three parts. First, you will diagnose what type of failure is happening. Second, you will decide how the app should respond. Third, you will design what the user actually sees.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a3666d4-8076-4e58-881b-97d81f707691",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83f9cc83-409d-480c-8787-9bc6c026de13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a4a650ac-bc88-4bd2-a8b2-e0cbdb415ef6",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *network failure*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *server failure*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *invalid data*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Highlight and compare examples of network failures, server failures, and invalid data.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Reinforce that different failures need different user messages. “Check your internet connection” makes sense for a network failure, but not for invalid data.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a4a650ac-bc88-4bd2-a8b2-e0cbdb415ef6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83f9cc83-409d-480c-8787-9bc6c026de13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "04498572-c98e-48d5-9ac2-11976544a61a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students identify failure types in a real-world ticket app scenario and translate user complaints into technical categories.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the When Things Break activity guide.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Diagnose Failures "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "04498572-c98e-48d5-9ac2-11976544a61a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83f9cc83-409d-480c-8787-9bc6c026de13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ddd8bed1-84fb-4726-a7d5-50ca81a7339e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1. In Level 1, students will read three user complaints from a concert ticketing app. For each one, use the user complaint and the system report to diagnose the failure type. Record your answer in the activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who confuse network and server failures. Ask: Is the problem that the request cannot travel, or that the server cannot respond?\n",
        "progression_name": "Diagnose Failures",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that user complaints usually describe what the user sees, not the exact technical cause. For the technical cause, they should refer to the system log messages.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ddd8bed1-84fb-4726-a7d5-50ca81a7339e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83f9cc83-409d-480c-8787-9bc6c026de13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "db1dbc54-8469-42f1-a46b-ca8a2e9b9cd2",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "db1dbc54-8469-42f1-a46b-ca8a2e9b9cd2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83f9cc83-409d-480c-8787-9bc6c026de13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "04a05b84-eb5c-4b6e-ac29-5c7667a9cf3e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students turn to the Fail Fast vs. Fail Quietly section of their activity guides. Have them discuss the prompt with a partner and record their opinion. As students discuss, listen for whether they can explain their choice in terms of trade-offs — there is no perfect answer here. Students should be able to articulate whether Fail Fast or Fail Quietly better serves user trust and why.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that 'User Trust' is the primary metric here. Ask: 'Which choice makes the user feel more in control and less confused?'\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Check for justifications that connect the technical strategy back to the human experience. For example: “Failing fast prevents the user from trying to buy a seat that is not available.”\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "04a05b84-eb5c-4b6e-ac29-5c7667a9cf3e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83f9cc83-409d-480c-8787-9bc6c026de13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9628e50-edd2-4475-b381-857112cf81e9",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students design user-facing solutions for technical failures using a reliability checklist and wireframe sketches.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Considering the User Experience"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9628e50-edd2-4475-b381-857112cf81e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83f9cc83-409d-480c-8787-9bc6c026de13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "064cabd0-f143-4ef8-a6ef-a887c29b6e2b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now it's time to design the fix. You've diagnosed the problem, now what does the user actually see? Decide if you want the page to fail fast or fail quietly and design an appropriate solution.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "064cabd0-f143-4ef8-a6ef-a887c29b6e2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83f9cc83-409d-480c-8787-9bc6c026de13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf81d590-02a4-44cd-84ba-76355a150f0f",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 2 to design the UI for a types of failures:\n* **Level 2:** Students brainstorm a recovery state design with AI Chat.\n* **Level 3:** Students use Sketch Lab to design a planned failure message for the ticketing app.\n* **Level 4:** Students response to a free-response question. \n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for “the action.” Every error message should give the user something to do, such as retry, refresh, check their connection, choose another seat, or contact support. If a student only writes, “It broke,” ask: What action should the user take now?\n",
        "progression_name": "Brainstorm With AI",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider distributing the (Optional) Designing for Failure activity guide for students to sketch out their ideas on paper.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Review students’ designs. A successful design includes non-technical language, a clear next step, and a 'calm' tone rather than an alarming or highly technical one.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf81d590-02a4-44cd-84ba-76355a150f0f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83f9cc83-409d-480c-8787-9bc6c026de13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88a1701c-b385-4e5f-8d3b-3f201f7e8ac7",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check For Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88a1701c-b385-4e5f-8d3b-3f201f7e8ac7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83f9cc83-409d-480c-8787-9bc6c026de13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4ef134cd-f400-4501-a73e-24b11db339e3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4ef134cd-f400-4501-a73e-24b11db339e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e597e6cf-c3a4-4f03-ab5a-17fba61a0980"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e85e262-7573-4afa-85fa-a2cc3236b67b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students analyze how different failure experiences affect user trust and begin recognizing that good apps are designed to handle errors intentionally, not accidentally.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Rank the three failure screens from most to least trustworthy. Explain your ranking.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that how a system fails matters as much as whether it fails. A friendly fallback communicates planning and care, a blank screen communicates neglect or instability, and exposed technical errors communicate lack of user-centered design. Students should begin to see trust as a product of intentional error handling.\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may initially rank based on aesthetics rather than trust signals. Push them to articulate why one feels more trustworthy — listen for language around clarity, intention, and user awareness. These ideas will anchor the vocabulary introduced later.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e85e262-7573-4afa-85fa-a2cc3236b67b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8093cb6-68c4-447a-bacb-efdd6121e8ab"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "778c58c5-b822-483d-bb47-32c94870e303",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *When nothing appears on the screen, what questions start running through your head as a user?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that a blank screen creates uncertainty and loss of control. Without feedback, users may blame themselves, their device, or the app. Students should understand that silence is not neutral, it is a communication failure, and that good systems provide feedback even when something goes wrong.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What message does this error screen send about the reliability of the system?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that exposing raw technical errors reduces perceived reliability and trust. Even if the issue is minor, showing stack traces or code suggests poor planning and lack of audience awareness. Students should understand that error messages must match the user’s level of understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why does the cat screen make the failure feel less frustrating, even though the problem isn't actually fixed?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that tone, visuals, and clear messaging regulate user emotion and preserve trust. A fallback does not fix the underlying technical problem, but it protects the user experience by communicating awareness and offering guidance. Students should connect this to intentional design through try/catch.\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Avoid introducing the term \"try/catch\" during this discussion — let students sit with the design observation. The goal here is building intuition; the vocabulary comes in the Activity.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "778c58c5-b822-483d-bb47-32c94870e303",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8093cb6-68c4-447a-bacb-efdd6121e8ab"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "18e7b053-d574-4638-8145-ac580d487c9a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "18e7b053-d574-4638-8145-ac580d487c9a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8093cb6-68c4-447a-bacb-efdd6121e8ab"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd31b9ae-73a0-4e17-8400-6159087dbb84",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students experience what happens when fetch code has no error handling, then connect that failure to the user experience — building the need for try/catch before vocabulary is introduced.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 8, Level 1 by predicting and then running code with a broken URL to see it crash.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Predict, Run, and Reflect",
        "progression_name": "Predict, Run, and Reflect",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage them to write a genuine prediction rather than a placeholder — even a brief one — so the run feels like a real test of their thinking.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd31b9ae-73a0-4e17-8400-6159087dbb84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0f2618b-4253-41d7-85f8-419e7e8d70d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "967efffd-c24b-433a-8411-b0d122478275",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\nWhen an app tries to load something and it fails, what should the user see? \nWhy is that better than showing nothing or showing a technical error?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect the failed request they just observed to the user experience. Listen for students noticing that users need clear feedback when something goes wrong, not a blank screen or raw error text. This sets up the idea that error handling should protect the user experience, not just prevent code from crashing.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "967efffd-c24b-433a-8411-b0d122478275",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0f2618b-4253-41d7-85f8-419e7e8d70d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "18362d0d-56dc-43a6-926f-6ff1d7639987",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build conceptual understanding of try, catch, and fallback through the trapeze metaphor and annotated code, using the unit guide to anchor key vocabulary before applying it in the Chaos Challenge.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Have students get out their copy of the Designing Reliable Apps with AI and APIs Unit Guide and turn to Lesson 8.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the trapeze metaphor using the three points on the slide. Connect each point to what students just observed in Level 1 — the crash they saw was the show ending without a net.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Building the Safety Net ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use this metaphor to emphasize that errors aren't always the programmer's fault—sometimes the \"rope\" (network) just breaks, and we have to be ready for it.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "18362d0d-56dc-43a6-926f-6ff1d7639987",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0f2618b-4253-41d7-85f8-419e7e8d70d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cfde9bda-f3ab-49b8-844a-e96a10545405",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the try block and direct students to fill in the definition in their unit guide. Connect back to the trapeze metaphor — the code inside the try block is the \"dangerous stunt,\" the part of the program that might fail. Ask students: \"In Level 1, which line of code do you think was the risky one?\" This helps students identify the fetch call as the code that belongs inside a try block.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the catch block and direct students to fill in the definition in their unit guide. Connect back to the trapeze metaphor — the catch block is the safety net waiting below. Emphasize that the net only activates when something goes wrong; if the try block succeeds, the catch block is skipped entirely. Ask students: \"In Level 1, what did the app do when the fetch failed — was there a net?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define fallback and direct students to fill in the definition in their unit guide. Connect back to the warm up — the cat screen students ranked as most trustworthy was a fallback. Now they have the vocabulary for what they already recognized instinctively. Ask students: \"What made the cat screen a good fallback compared to the blank screen or the error message?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to reveal each annotation one at a time. As each label appears, connect it back to the vocabulary students just filled in — the try block wraps the risky fetch code, the catch block handles the failure, and line 6 is the fallback. Have students annotate the code segment in their unit guide as you walk through it.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cfde9bda-f3ab-49b8-844a-e96a10545405",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0f2618b-4253-41d7-85f8-419e7e8d70d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c79d9aaa-1ff0-4325-8409-8c9d8debf18e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply their understanding of try, catch, and fallback by choosing and implementing a fallback strategy in a realistic error scenario, connecting code decisions to user experience outcomes.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "The Chaos Challenge "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c79d9aaa-1ff0-4325-8409-8c9d8debf18e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0f2618b-4253-41d7-85f8-419e7e8d70d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b409dd9a-68f0-4e8d-a1b1-faa798ba1f8f",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": " It's Launch Day, and everything is going wrong! The API is down, and users are starting to complain. You are the Crisis Manager. You need to choose one of three problems and fix it so our users don't abandon the app.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b409dd9a-68f0-4e8d-a1b1-faa798ba1f8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0f2618b-4253-41d7-85f8-419e7e8d70d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a62c31b5-e69c-4e89-ae6d-42a1a8f08cb8",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly describe the three options students can choose from — fixing the error message language (The Apology), adding a fallback image, or adding a Retry button — and direct students to pick the one that interests them most before moving to the levels."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a62c31b5-e69c-4e89-ae6d-42a1a8f08cb8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0f2618b-4253-41d7-85f8-419e7e8d70d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ad432219-272a-460d-996e-76cf39cc70ef",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 2 to complete Levels 2-5.\n* **Level 2:** Students modify the code by wrapping the fetch in a try block and adding a catch block that prints to the console.\n* **Level 3:** Students read the three \"Chaos\" scenarios.\n* **Level 4:** Students choose a fallback strategy and update the catch block to show a more helpful response when the fetch fails.\n* **Level 5:** Students demonstrate their understanding of try...catch by explaining how a fallback message changes the user experience when an API request fails.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who forget the curly braces for the catch block — remind them that catch needs its own block of code to run. Ask students whether the red error still stops the whole program now that they've added the catch block.\n",
        "progression_name": "Chaos Challenge",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 5 can be used as a formative assessment of the objective Describe how try and catch contributes to defensive design. Check that students have placed their fallback logic (the apology, image, or button) inside the catch block, not the try block, as evidence of the objective Wrap async fetch logic in try and catch blocks.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ad432219-272a-460d-996e-76cf39cc70ef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0f2618b-4253-41d7-85f8-419e7e8d70d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27f9043d-47dc-4d86-81fa-cee62a7d3c88",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How does using try and catch make your app more trustworthy for users?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that even if a feature fails, a controlled error message maintains user trust better than a crash or technical jargon. Even if the data doesn't load, a polite message or fallback image keeps the user feeling safe and in control.\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may give surface responses like \"it doesn't crash.\" Push them to connect to the user — ask what the user feels or thinks when they see a polite fallback versus a blank screen or an error. Listen for language around trust, clarity, and intention, which echoes the warm up discussion and sets up the Wrap Up vocabulary review.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27f9043d-47dc-4d86-81fa-cee62a7d3c88",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d0f2618b-4253-41d7-85f8-419e7e8d70d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f086392a-e841-491e-9389-521abca0cf93",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to answer using a specific example from the lesson — the crash they saw in Level 1, the fallback they built in the Chaos Challenge, or the annotated code from the slide. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out. Listen for students connecting try and catch to both preventing crashes and protecting the user experience.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f086392a-e841-491e-9389-521abca0cf93",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a2cd29b-d4e9-49e7-9b8c-e3d04ce2b979"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53f9b80d-0385-411c-a673-5069f2f75fcd",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53f9b80d-0385-411c-a673-5069f2f75fcd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1a2cd29b-d4e9-49e7-9b8c-e3d04ce2b979"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "07036994-c17d-4377-a7d7-21ea7e52af23",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explore how loading screens communicate app state through visual cues, building intuition for why user-facing feedback matters before the concept of human-centered reliability is introduced."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "07036994-c17d-4377-a7d7-21ea7e52af23",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2f0dd913-425f-4d78-9d14-a7c8f560889d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f0e891c-5191-47c6-a17b-0d055e643a17",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you think is loading here? How do you know?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that the shape and layout of the gray boxes (the 'skeleton') mimics the layout of a social media profile. Students should realize that even without text, certain shapes suggest specific types of content. Because gray boxes tell your brain what to expect, you aren't staring at a blank screen; you're looking at a layout that is about to be filled.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Push students to go beyond just saying the app is loading. Have them be specific about what kind of content they would expect to see. If students struggle, ask them to think about the apps they use most often.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f0e891c-5191-47c6-a17b-0d055e643a17",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2f0dd913-425f-4d78-9d14-a7c8f560889d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3bcb5478-a5ca-48bc-ad05-dea251e44631",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3bcb5478-a5ca-48bc-ad05-dea251e44631",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2f0dd913-425f-4d78-9d14-a7c8f560889d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08265f2a-2420-42fc-b3d6-45306e66759f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What do you think is loading here?*\n- *Why include text as well as a progress bar?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students understand that the progress bar manages patience by showing time remaining, while the text communicates that the app is actively working on specific tasks behind the scenes. Without the bar, you don't know how long it will take. Without the text, you don't know if the app is actually doing anything useful.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that this loading screen also features a preview of the costumes that users will likely see on the loaded screen. This helps users anticipate what they are about to see and manages their expectations for waiting time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08265f2a-2420-42fc-b3d6-45306e66759f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2f0dd913-425f-4d78-9d14-a7c8f560889d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "643a37ce-7773-43a4-905f-40b1209daf4b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "643a37ce-7773-43a4-905f-40b1209daf4b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2f0dd913-425f-4d78-9d14-a7c8f560889d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ddef6117-62ad-4cbc-81d5-7e73602105b3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What do you think is loading here?*\n- *Why does this represent poor design?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students understand that without any context — no text, no progress bar, no skeleton layout — the user has no way of knowing what's happening, how long it will take, or what to expect. Poor design erodes user trust. Apps that communicate clearly keep users informed and in control.\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students say it \"looks bad\" or \"is confusing,\" push them further: What specifically does the user not know? How might that affect whether they trust the app? This sets up the concept of human-centered reliability introduced right after.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ddef6117-62ad-4cbc-81d5-7e73602105b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2f0dd913-425f-4d78-9d14-a7c8f560889d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ac121e8-3023-43a9-8f4d-5aa1352604fe",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ac121e8-3023-43a9-8f4d-5aa1352604fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2f0dd913-425f-4d78-9d14-a7c8f560889d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09cff89a-9fed-442a-9834-4b1d4c31d64e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students are introduced to the three possible outcomes of a page load — loading, empty, and error states — and connect them to the concept of human-centered reliability before applying these ideas in the Unit Guide and on Code Studio.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Have students get out their Designing Reliable Apps with AI and APIs Unit Guide and turn to Lesson 9.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that every time your app makes a request to an API, it needs to handle three scenarios. You never want to leave a user looking at a blank screen or a broken page. Click through the animated slide to explain each state: the loading state, empty state, and error state. Remind students that loading the page perfectly, i.e. following the “Happy Path, is only one possible outcome for the user experience. Reliability comes from handling the other paths well so the user never feels confused or upset.\n\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Introduction to the Three States ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "An empty state and an error state are easy to confuse. A good empty state tells the user why there is no data being displayed. An error state communicates that something went wrong and the user will need to try again. Push students to be precise about the difference.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09cff89a-9fed-442a-9834-4b1d4c31d64e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "37053ffc-7e23-46d6-ae37-d198dcef63fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af8e70ac-0551-4cb5-9222-dd8ef9d0a59a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define human-centered reliability and have students fill in the vocabulary section in their Unit Guide. Connect back to the warm up by pointing out that each loading screen example demonstrated this concept in action — the skeleton screen, the progress bar with text, and the bare spinner all showed different levels of transparency with the user."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af8e70ac-0551-4cb5-9222-dd8ef9d0a59a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "37053ffc-7e23-46d6-ae37-d198dcef63fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5126359b-9381-46d9-9ab3-f12ac80ab96f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students design and implement loading, empty, and error states for a web app, test their implementation with a partner, and reflect on how these states support human-centered reliability.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 1 to complete Levels 1-4.\n* **Level 1:** Students use Sketch Lab to design their three states.\n* **Level 2:** Students implement their loading, empty, and error states in Web Lab and test their work.\n* **Level 3:** Students test a partner’s app by forcing the data array to be empty and checking if their \"vibe\" holds up\n* **Level 4:** Students answer reflection questions to demonstrate their understanding of human-centered reliability.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are correctly placing their innerHTML updates before the fetch call begins (for the loading state).\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Human-Centered Reliability ",
        "progression_name": "Human-Centered Reliability",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The program on Level 3 is designed to choose an outcome randomly. Make sure students keep clicking \"Find flights\" and resetting the results until they have tested all three states.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The levels can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objectives. \n\nOn Level 2, check that students have successfully implemented a visible loading indicator that appears as soon as the button is clicked. Check that students have implemented an empty state and error state.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5126359b-9381-46d9-9ab3-f12ac80ab96f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "37053ffc-7e23-46d6-ae37-d198dcef63fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eaf82bcd-93cd-4d36-9c79-d00ffedfdfed",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answer using a specific example from the lesson — a loading, empty, or error state they implemented, or a choice they made in their design. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. Listen for students connecting their design and implementation decisions back to the idea of keeping users informed.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eaf82bcd-93cd-4d36-9c79-d00ffedfdfed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a60a8286-4646-4a53-a6b6-aab8104ff526"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c4a20d06-1d5a-4be4-a090-afd77ccc7950",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c4a20d06-1d5a-4be4-a090-afd77ccc7950",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a60a8286-4646-4a53-a6b6-aab8104ff526"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "abba226d-81b9-4864-ac09-9f3a371fda2b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build an intuitive understanding of the tradeoffs between local and live data sources — framed through the real decision-making a Data Engineer faces when an app goes down."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "abba226d-81b9-4864-ac09-9f3a371fda2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6f29cce2-a5a0-4d7c-a6ef-7c67f343567c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a28769b7-5c39-43e4-a67c-25903b535f43",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Data Engineers are the people responsible for making sure apps always have the data they need — no matter what. One of the most important decisions they make is: where should this data live? Should it be hardcoded into the app, or fetched live from the internet? Today, you're going to think like a Data Engineer. Here's the situation: A Data Engineer at a sports app just got paged at 2am. The app is showing a blank screen — no scores, no stats, nothing. The live API that serves game data went down.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a28769b7-5c39-43e4-a67c-25903b535f43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6f29cce2-a5a0-4d7c-a6ef-7c67f343567c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5b26ce3-9de9-4cf6-adfd-1cbeda4c77c9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompt. \n\n*For each item, decide: Local (hardcoded) or Live (fetched):*\n* *Live NBA Game Scores*\n* *Team Logo Image*\n* *List of Sports Categories*\n* *Weather Forecast for Game Day*\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that frequently changing, time-sensitive data (scores, weather) should be fetched Live, while stable, structural data (team logos, sport category lists) are better stored locally for speed and reliability. Push students to articulate why — a Data Engineer (whose job is to make those decisions before something breaks) chooses Local for instant load times and reliability, and Live for real-time updates and dynamic content. This is the core tradeoff Data Engineers navigate every time they design a data system. \n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What pattern do you notice in the solution below?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify that frequently changing, time-sensitive data should be fetched Live, while stable, structural, or rarely changing data is better stored locally for speed and reliability.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Why not make the Home icon Live?*\n- *Why not make the NBA scores Local?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students articulate why developers choose Local data for stability and instant load times, and Live data for real-time updates and dynamic content.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5b26ce3-9de9-4cf6-adfd-1cbeda4c77c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6f29cce2-a5a0-4d7c-a6ef-7c67f343567c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "864c1892-a58a-4b9d-9c23-1ec4fd0bc203",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn what a Data Engineer does, then simulate the role by building a hybrid app that uses both local and live data to stay functional even when an API fails.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Data Engineer career.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1 to learn about the Data Engineer career.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students move through the panels, listen for students who are already connecting the career to apps they use. These students can be cold-called during the discussion on the next slide. Also note any students who are rushing through — the panels are short but the discussion will expect them to recall specific details.\n\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Meet the Career: Data Engineer",
        "progression_name": "Meet the Career: Data Engineer",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may conflate \"Data Engineer\" with \"Data Scientist.\" If this comes up, a quick distinction: Data Scientists analyze data to find insights; Data Engineers build the systems that make sure the data is available, reliable, and in the right place to begin with.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "864c1892-a58a-4b9d-9c23-1ec4fd0bc203",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b837c16f-ef91-4c7e-9bb9-0d3c5c6b5a4a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de772490-c106-4839-bdee-ade270350cb6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n* *What does a Data Engineer do, and why does it matter?*\n* *How did you see Data Engineers using AI in their work?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students can describe the Data Engineer role in their own words and identify at least one way Data Engineers use AI tools in their work. This discussion bridges the career introduction to the coding work ahead and primes students to use AI Chat intentionally in the levels.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de772490-c106-4839-bdee-ade270350cb6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b837c16f-ef91-4c7e-9bb9-0d3c5c6b5a4a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bf78dcae-bd16-4865-92cf-fb00a65c263a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you will act as Data Engineers. Your job is to build a Global Population Tracker — an app that fetches live country population data from the internet. But like any real app, it needs to keep working even when the live source goes down. You'll set up the app's \"shell\" - the static structure that loads instantly while the app waits for live data, create a backup, and write the logic that decides which data to use. You can also use AI Tutor to help you understand what the API data looks like and build backup data that matches its structure.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The warm-up used a sports app to introduce the concept — this app is different, but the Data Engineer's decisions are exactly the same. If students notice the switch, that's actually worth acknowledging: Data Engineers apply the same local vs. live thinking to any app they build, regardless of what the data is about.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bf78dcae-bd16-4865-92cf-fb00a65c263a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b837c16f-ef91-4c7e-9bb9-0d3c5c6b5a4a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "36bd106d-62e6-4de7-911c-0afea0836ded",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Every app you use relies on data. The real question is: where does that data come from, and what happens if it’s not available?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "36bd106d-62e6-4de7-911c-0afea0836ded",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b837c16f-ef91-4c7e-9bb9-0d3c5c6b5a4a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31aac166-d071-43c7-8dd6-5d7e611d15dd",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *live data*. Remind students that in the warm-up, the NBA scores and weather forecast were examples of live data — they had to be fetched because they change constantly. When a Data Engineer connects an app to an external server to get that kind of real-time data, they're working with live data. Direct students to fill in the vocab term in their Unit Guides.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *local data*. Point back to the warm-up — the team logo and list of sport categories were examples of local data. They don't change often, so there's no need to fetch them. A Data Engineer hardcodes that kind of data directly into the app so it loads instantly, every time, regardless of internet connection. Direct students to fill in the vocab term in their Unit Guides.\n\n\n\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students sometimes assume local data is always \"worse\" because it can go stale. Push back on this — for stable, structural data, local is actually the better choice. Speed and reliability are features, not compromises.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31aac166-d071-43c7-8dd6-5d7e611d15dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b837c16f-ef91-4c7e-9bb9-0d3c5c6b5a4a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2ad3c510-ab6e-4ae5-a03c-ff1eb906f584",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Data Engineers weigh four factors when deciding where data should live: speed, reliability, control, and whether the data needs to be up-to-date.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2ad3c510-ab6e-4ae5-a03c-ff1eb906f584",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b837c16f-ef91-4c7e-9bb9-0d3c5c6b5a4a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca0a385e-5245-46c6-bb2d-e042c6c51aee",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through each factor and compare how local and live data handle it. Ask students which column describes the NBA scores from the warm-up, and which describes the team logo. Students should recognize that neither approach is universally better — the right choice depends on what the data is and how it's used.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume live data is always better because it's more current. Push back on this — for stable, structural data, local is faster, more reliable, and fully controlled by the engineer. The tradeoff is always intentional, not a compromise.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca0a385e-5245-46c6-bb2d-e042c6c51aee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b837c16f-ef91-4c7e-9bb9-0d3c5c6b5a4a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c0f9ad26-342c-45d4-a63a-44027267663a",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Now that students can see how local and live data compare, zoom in on the two specific ways Data Engineers use local data in their apps. Job 1 addresses speed and control. Data Engineers always set up the app shell first because it loads instantly, no internet is needed, and the developer owns the data completely. Job 2 addresses reliability — backup data keeps the app functional when the live source fails. Point out the up-to-date tradeoff: backup data is a safety net, but it may not reflect the latest changes. This is a real risk Data Engineers have to account for. Together, these two uses of local data form what developers call an app shell — the static structure that loads instantly before any live data arrives. Students will build this in Level 2 and create the backup data in Level 3.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Backup data must have the exact same structure as the real API data — the same keys, like name and population. If the structure doesn't match, the code will break when it switches to the backup.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c0f9ad26-342c-45d4-a63a-44027267663a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b837c16f-ef91-4c7e-9bb9-0d3c5c6b5a4a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe7aa2da-494a-42be-93a5-55637b153e0a",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will design a resilient hybrid web app that combines static configuration, live API data, and local backup data — demonstrating how real-world Data Engineers handle both dynamic content and failure conditions.\n\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 5.\n* **Level 2:** Students initialize the static UI using an appConfig object.\n* **Level 3:** Students create the backupData array to match the API structure.\n* **Level 4:** Students implement try/catch logic to switch to backup data if the fetch fails.\n* **Level 5:** Students complete a Check for Understanding question\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:**  Check that students' backup data in Level 3 uses the same keys (name and population) as the live API response. This is the most common point of failure in Level 4.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Build Like a Data Engineer ",
        "progression_name": "Build Like a Data Engineer",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students finish early, ask them: \"Your app works great now — but what if the backup data also became outdated? How would you solve that as a Data Engineer?\" This pushes early finishers to think beyond the lesson without requiring additional code.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 4 is the culmination of the activity and can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objectives:\n* Compare local arrays and objects with data from an external API — look for students successfully switching between localCountries and live API data\n* Analyze tradeoffs in speed, control, reliability, and having up to date data — look for students who can explain why the app shell loads first and why backup data is a safety net\n* Identify risks of depending on an external API, including service failure and data being up to date— look for students whose try/catch logic correctly handles a failed fetch without breaking the app\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe7aa2da-494a-42be-93a5-55637b153e0a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b837c16f-ef91-4c7e-9bb9-0d3c5c6b5a4a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e04ed013-3120-4aee-95a6-4626f78b2545",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their answers. Encourage them to address both halves — why a Data Engineer would choose live data over local data (real-time updates, dynamic content) and what they built to keep the app running when the live source fails (the app shell and backup data). Encourage students to use examples from their own app in Levels 2 through 4, or the sports app scenario from the warm-up. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. Listen for students using the terms live data, local data, app shell, backup data, and fallback in their responses.\n\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e04ed013-3120-4aee-95a6-4626f78b2545",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1fe98b09-0dd9-434c-a6d9-2bbdfa88a420"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2bc77620-099d-48f7-875b-2e23679d1da8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the key vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2bc77620-099d-48f7-875b-2e23679d1da8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1fe98b09-0dd9-434c-a6d9-2bbdfa88a420"
      }
    },
    {
